            <ArticleTitle>Psychosocial care needs of children with recent-onset asthma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>3-12</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To examine psychosocial care needs of children with recent-onset asthma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN/METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Data were collected over 2 years from 63 children ages 8-14 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Children's need for attention to specific aspects of their asthma care remained high over the 2 years, as did their perceived needs for information and support and their concerns and fears.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PRACTICE IMPLICATIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Children have many needs, and healthcare professionals may not be providing care that addresses these needs. The Child Report of Psychosocial Care can be used as a quick tool to assess and guide interventions related to specific areas of need.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
